Hi guys,

since the future package was updated to 1.40.0, the tidyverse logic is not working for `furrr::future_map()` anymore. Please see my hyothetical code chunk below, which was working with the 1.34.0 version. 

```
library("datasets")
library("tidyverse")
library("purrr")
library("future")

df = datasets::mtcars

# create 100 bootstrap samples for varying mean
set.seed(1234)
boot_df <- map(seq_len(100), function(x){
  dplyr::sample_n(df, nrow(df), replace = TRUE) %>% 
    mutate(none = 0)
})

# create mean function
mean_function <- function(data, item) {
    dplyr::reframe(data, mw = mean({{ item }}))
}

# create mean_function_future that uses future_map()
mean_function_future <- function(data_list, item) {
    future::plan(multisession, workers = 4, gc = TRUE)

    res <- furrr::future_map(data_list, function(x) {
        mean_function(x, {{ item }})
    }, .options = furrr::furrr_options(globals = c("mean_function")))

    return(res)
}

g = mean_function_future(boot_df, mpg)
```

Does anyone have an idea on how to fix the code chunk that it is working again? 
Thank you for your help 😊

Hello,

I was running furrr::future_map() for different functions that manage tryCatch errors and I noticed a problem (I do not know if it will happen in the other furrr functions).

If the tryCatch function print the error as a message: 

`error = function(e){
      message(e);
      return(NA)
      }`
      
  furrr::future_map() stops from that point. It cannot manage the error, but if you comment the `message(e)` then it works. 
  
  I attach a reproducible example:
  
```
lst <- list(1,2,3,-4,"a","b")

future::plan("multisession", workers = 2)

logFunction <- function(n){
  
  res <- tryCatch(
    expr = {
      exp(n)
    },
    # Specifying error message
    error = function(e){
      message(e) #if you comment this line works
      return(NA)
    }
  )
}

res_vector <- furrr::future_map(lst, ~logFunction(.))
```

Thanks for developing the furrr package.

Regards,
Pedro Salguero

